  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NIHR HPSR-CA - Improving primary health care access and quality in Georgia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Primary health care (PHC) is a community&apos;s first point of contact with the health system. Many countries are trying to improve PHC, especially as people are getting older and have more health problems. This is particularly important for countries such as Georgia where long-term health problems such as heart disease or diabetes are common and where PHC is under-developed. 

Georgia plans to change its PHC system to make it easier to access, especially for people in rural areas. Currently, some community members find it difficult to get to see a health worker because there are too few or they are overworked, while others are referred to specialists for care that could be provide at the primary level. Also, patients often have to pay directly for services, including for medicines. To address these challenges Georgia is introducing a package of reforms to improve PHC financing, organisation and quality. This project will investigate how well these changes are being conducted and whether they lead to better health services. It is well known from other countries, how challenging such comprehensive change can be. This project will systematically investigate in real-time how well these reforms are realised. This will give decision-makers and practitioners important information about how to adjust reform plans, ensure that the changes are accepted by the population, and that they will last. It will also generate new knowledge to help other countries that want to reform their PHC systems. The research will be carried out in three regions in Georgia that are experiencing different problems in accessing PHC. 

The project is undertaken by a team of researchers in Georgia (Curatio International Foundation, Ilia State University) and the UK (London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine). The team will be supported by the &apos;World Health Organisation&#x2019;s European Centre for Primary Health Care&apos;. The work will be carried out over four years, from November 2024 to October 2028.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project has 5 objectives:

1. To assess the experiences of individuals with diabetes, hypertension, and/or chronic respiratory diseases regarding their utilisation of primary health care (PHC) services, and to evaluate the extent to which PHC reform enhances the quality of care they receive.
2. To explore the experiences of healthcare providers with the new healthcare delivery methods introduced by the PHC reform, focusing on changes in their practices and their responses to new payment models for services rendered.
3. To examine the understanding of PHC reform among decision-makers, and to evaluate their readiness and commitment to adopting changes in their operational practices as a result of the reform.
4. To analyse the influence of the broader political and economic context in Georgia on the implementation and effectiveness of the PHC reform.
5. To investigate whether the reforms lead to improved equitable and affordable access to PHC services, and to identify variations in access across different regions of Georgia.</narrative>
  </description>
